======================================================================================================================
k_cross-scale_opencluster -- CANDIDATE 2: the open-cluster rung of the external-field saturation law
      M_dyn/M_phot = B(e) = nu(e)[1+L(e)/3] at the solar circle,  a_0 = (c/2) sqrt(G rho_DE)
======================================================================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0.  THE VIRIAL COEFFICIENT, derived here rather than remembered (it multiplies every mass).
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] the Plummer potential energy integrates to the analytic -3 pi G M^2/(32 b)  [CAN FAIL]   (numeric -0.294524 vs analytic -0.294524 (units G=M=b=1))
  [PASS] the Plummer PROJECTED half-mass radius is b exactly, so eta = 32/pi refers to r50 as measured  [CAN FAIL]   (numeric R_hp/b = 1.00000)
  => M_dyn = eta sigma_1D^2 r50 / G with eta = 32/pi = 10.1859 (Plummer).
  SYSTEMATIC, stated up front: eta is profile-dependent.  A King W0 = 5 model gives eta ~ 8.5 and a
  mass-segregated cluster measured at the half-NUMBER radius (which r50J is) gives a larger r50 than the
  half-MASS radius, both of which move the answer by 15-25%.  That is 0.06-0.10 dex -- the SAME SIZE as
  the whole predicted signal, and it is quoted as a floor, not hidden.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  THE SAMPLE.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  7167 clusters in Hunt & Reffert 2024 table 1; 401 member files fetched (type o, d < 1000 pc, N_J >= 50, prob_J > 0.5)
  393 clusters keep >= 40 members after inrj = 1, Prob > 0.7, RUWE < 1.4, NSS = 0
  [PASS] the sample is large enough to be a rung at all (>= 30 clusters)  [CAN FAIL]   (393 clusters)
  sum of member Mass50 within r_J divided by the catalogue's MassJ: median 0.581 [0.400, 1.146]
  [PASS] MassJ carries a real unseen-star (IMF extrapolation) correction rather than being the bare sum of detected member masses -- which is the difference between a photometric mass and a fitted one  [CAN FAIL]   (median ratio 0.581, i.e. MassJ = 1.72x the summed detected member masses)
  THIS CHECK REVERSED AN ASSUMPTION AND THE REVERSAL IS LOAD-BEARING.  The catalogue mass is NOT the sum of
  what Gaia sees; it is that sum times an IMF extrapolation to the undetected low-mass end, a factor 1.72
  on the median cluster.  So M_phot is a FITTED quantity with an IMF normalisation inside it, and the
  Upsilon lever of section 9 is not a hypothetical: the entire difference between B = 0.6 and B = 1.0 below
  is that one extrapolation.  Section 9 quantifies it by re-running.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  DOES THE ESTIMATOR WORK?  Synthetic clusters with a KNOWN sigma, built on the REAL
    positions, REAL proper-motion errors, REAL bulk motions and a REAL line-of-sight depth.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     input sigma = 0.30 km/s:  recovered PERP 0.290 [0.254, 0.312];  recovered PARALLEL 0.327 [0.296, 0.399]
     input sigma = 1.00 km/s:  recovered PERP 0.958 [0.873, 1.022];  recovered PARALLEL 1.018 [0.945, 1.083]
  [PASS] the PERPENDICULAR estimator is unbiased to 15% at both 0.3 and 1.0 km/s, so a measured boost is not an estimator artefact  [CAN FAIL]   (recovered/input = 0.965 at 0.3 km/s, 0.958 at 1.0 km/s)
  [PASS] the PARALLEL component is measurably MORE biased by the line-of-sight depth than the perpendicular one, which is the reason the perpendicular one is used  [CAN FAIL]   (parallel/input = 1.089 vs perpendicular/input = 0.965 at sigma = 0.3 km/s)

