----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.  THE DATA OVERTURN THE MONTE CARLO: sigma_bin CANNOT be 0.55 km/s.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  335/392 clusters have sigma_perp < the Monte-Carlo floor 0.548 km/s, and the 5th percentile of sigma_perp is 0.219 km/s.
  A floor is a FLOOR: no cluster can sit below it.  So the Monte Carlo is an OVER-estimate, and by how much
  is set by the data themselves.  Two reasons it over-estimates, both real:
    (i)  it used m1 = 1 Msun; the median catalogue member mass here is 0.78 Msun, and v_orb ~ sqrt(M);
    (ii) HDBSCAN membership and the Prob > 0.7 cut are THEMSELVES a velocity cut: a star whose photocentre
         is displaced by >1 km/s from the cluster mean is a less probable member and is trimmed.
  [PASS] the Monte-Carlo binary floor is falsified by the data, which is why it is reported as a bound and not used as a correction  [CAN FAIL]   (5th percentile sigma_perp = 0.219 < MC floor 0.548 km/s)
  EMPIRICAL bound instead: sigma_floor <= 0.219 km/s.  Section 7 FITS it.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7.  THE ESTIMATOR THAT ACTUALLY SEPARATES THE HYPOTHESES.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every confound named above -- binaries, a non-equilibrium expansion velocity, membership trimming -- adds
  a velocity variance that does NOT scale with the cluster's virial dispersion.  The boost multiplies one
  that DOES.  Over 1.4 dex of cluster mass those two separate:
          sigma_obs^2 = B x G M_phot/(eta r50) + sigma_floor^2,
  with B the enclosed-mass boost the law predicts and sigma_floor^2 everything else.  Fit BOTH.

  ALL 392 clusters, inverse-variance weighted:
     B          =    0.736 +- 0.046      [Newton 1.000; saturation law 1.269 (canonical) / 1.344 (alt); isolated MOND 5.60]
     sigma_floor =    0.225 km/s   (fitted, not assumed;  floor variance +0.0507 +- 0.0030 km^2/s^2)

  subset                                      N         B      +-  sigma_floor   sigma(B) vs signal
  all                                       392     0.736   0.046        0.225                  0.2
  M_phot > 300 Msun (floor subdominant)     152     0.621   0.054        0.255                  0.2
  M_phot > 1000 Msun                         39     0.445   0.082        0.329                  0.3
  age > 100 Myr (dynamically relaxed)       185     0.647   0.048        0.249                  0.2
  age > 100 Myr and M_phot > 300             91     0.664   0.054        0.243                  0.2
  d < 500 pc (best astrometry)              122     0.766   0.077        0.200                  0.3
  r50 < 5 pc (compact, tides weak)          375     0.724   0.048        0.224                  0.2
  the last column is the fit's error bar divided by the whole predicted signal (B - 1 = 0.27): a value
  above 1 means the rung CANNOT see the law even in principle on this data set.
  [PASS] the joint fit is DECISIVE against isolated MOND -- the restatement branch, which is all that v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra gives -- at >= 3 sigma  [CAN FAIL]   (B = 0.664 +- 0.054 vs isolated MOND's 5.60: 90.7 sigma)
  [FAIL] [canonical] the fitted B is within 3 sigma of the SATURATION law's prediction  [CAN FAIL]   (B = 0.664 +- 0.054 vs 1.269: 11.1 sigma)
  [FAIL] [alt] the fitted B is within 3 sigma of the SATURATION law's prediction  [CAN FAIL]   (B = 0.664 +- 0.054 vs 1.344: 12.5 sigma)
  [FAIL] the fitted B is within 3 sigma of NEWTON's 1.000  [CAN FAIL]   (B = 0.664 +- 0.054: 6.2 sigma from 1)

