8.  IS THAT FIT ROBUST?  Three estimators of the same two numbers, and a degeneracy check.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  subset: age > 100 Myr and M_phot > 300 Msun, N = 91
     inverse-variance linear fit   B =   0.664 +- 0.054   sigma_floor = 0.243 km/s
     unweighted linear fit         B =   0.561 +- 0.059   sigma_floor = 0.312 km/s
     BINNED-MEDIAN fit (primary)   B =   0.590 +- 0.077   sigma_floor = 0.292 km/s
     bootstrap of the binned fit   B =   0.589 +- 0.139   (400 resamples)

   bin     N  sigma_vir^2 [km2/s2]  sigma_obs^2 [km2/s2]    ratio
     1    12                0.0356                0.1077     3.03
     2    11                0.0450                0.0848     1.89
     3    11                0.0571                0.1517     2.66
     4    12                0.0696                0.1338     1.92
     5    12                0.0896                0.1407     1.57
     6    11                0.1302                0.1504     1.16
     7    11                0.1802                0.1791     0.99
     8    12                0.3170                0.2797     0.88
  [PASS] the three estimators of B agree within their errors, so the answer is not an artefact of the weighting [CAN FAIL]   (IV 0.664, OLS 0.561, binned 0.590, bootstrap 0.589 +- 0.139)
  DEGENERACY CHECK.  Forcing sigma_floor = 0 gives B = 1.056 instead of 0.590: the floor and the
  boost ARE anti-correlated, and that is the single biggest reason this rung cannot resolve a 27% boost.
  [PASS] B and sigma_floor are strongly degenerate, so a quoted B is only as good as the floor model  [CAN FAIL]   (floor free: B = 0.590;  floor = 0: B = 1.056)

