9.  WHAT MOVES B, MEASURED BY RE-RUNNING -- INCLUDING THE UPSILON LEVER.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  variation                                                       B   d log B / d log X
  baseline (Plummer eta, catalogue M_phot and r50)            0.590                  --
  Upsilon x 1.5 (IMF normalisation of M_phot)                 0.393              -1.000
  Upsilon x 0.667                                             0.884              -1.000
  eta = 8.5 (King W0 = 5 instead of Plummer)                  0.492               1.000
  r50 x 0.85 (half-MASS < half-NUMBER, mass segregation)      0.501               1.000
  M_phot x 1.25 (unseen low-mass stars)                       0.472              -1.000
  M_phot = bare SUM of detected member masses (no IMF extrapolation)    1.015              -1.000
  [PASS] the UPSILON LEVER is exactly -1: B is a ratio of a dynamical mass to a photometric one, so the whole amplitude of this rung rides on the IMF normalisation  [CAN FAIL]   (d log B/d log Upsilon = -1.000 (analytic -1.000))
  READ THAT AGAINST INTEREST AND BOTH WAYS.  To move the measured B up to the law's 1.27 needs M_phot
  54% SMALLER than the catalogue's; to reach Newton's 1.000 needs 41% smaller.
  And the LAST ROW of the table is not hypothetical: dropping the catalogue's IMF extrapolation entirely --
  using only the stellar mass Gaia actually detects -- moves B from 0.59 to 1.01, i.e. across BOTH
  Newton's prediction and most of the way to the law's.  ONE defensible modelling choice inside the
  photometric mass spans the entire discriminating range.  That is the rung's verdict in one line.

