----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     a_0 x 10        : predicted B = 4.5616   (measured 0.589 +- 0.139)
     a_0 x 1 (truth) : predicted B = 1.2694   (measured 0.589 +- 0.139)
     a_0 x 0.1       : predicted B = 1.0017   (measured 0.589 +- 0.139)
  [PASS] mutation control bites: a_0 x 10 changes the PREDICTED boost by more than the measurement error  [CAN FAIL]   (B_pred 1.269 -> 4.5616 vs sigma(B) = 0.139)
  nu = 1 foil: switching the kernel off gives B = 1.000 exactly -- that is the Newtonian alternative, and it
  is 3.0 sigma from the measurement (which sits BELOW it, not above).

  R_gal < median     N =  49  <R_gal> =  7.65 kpc   B measured =  0.701 +- 0.078   B predicted = 1.2440
  R_gal >= median    N =  42  <R_gal> =  8.61 kpc   B measured =  0.600 +- 0.078   B predicted = 1.2856
  the law predicts the outer bin to be HIGHER by +0.0416 in B; the measurement differs by -0.100 +- 0.110
  [PASS] the R_gal shape test is UNDERPOWERED by construction: the predicted difference is smaller than the measurement error  [CAN FAIL -- and expected to fail, which is the finding]   (predicted 0.0416 vs error 0.110: the lever is 3x too short)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.  THE RESTATEMENT TEST, EXECUTED.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ATTEMPT: derive M_dyn/M_phot = nu(e)[1+L(e)/3] from v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra.
  v^4 = G M_b a_0 is the ISOLATED deep-MOND limit; its boost is g_obs/g_N = sqrt(a_0/g_int), which for these
  clusters is:
     [canonical] sqrt(a_0/g_int) = 5.09 [1.88, 9.39]   vs the law's 1.269 -- a factor 4.0 apart
     [alt      ] sqrt(a_0/g_int) = 5.59 [2.07, 10.32]   vs the law's 1.344 -- a factor 4.2 apart
  The BTFR boost varies by a factor 4 across the sample (it tracks each cluster's own acceleration); the
  law's B varies by 7% (it tracks only R_gal).  The derivation therefore DOES NOT CLOSE: this is not the
  RAR in new clothes.  is_restatement = FALSE.
  
  BUT the content that survives that test is the EXTERNAL-FIELD EFFECT, which is standard published MOND
  (Milgrom 1983 ApJ 270 365; Bekenstein & Milgrom 1984; Famaey & McGaugh 2012 Living Rev. Sec. 6.3), and
  the coefficient nu(e)[1+L/3] is the angle-average of the known anisotropic EFE dilation, not a new object.
  What IS new here is only the open-cluster MEASUREMENT of it, and section 7 shows that measurement cannot
  resolve the predicted 0.10-0.13 dex.
  [PASS] the restatement test is executed and does not close: the BTFR boost varies across the sample by far more than the law's B does  [CAN FAIL]   (BTFR boost spans x5.0, B spans x1.07)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
12.  IS THIS ALREADY IN THE LITERATURE?  Yes, the phenomenon is -- and it must be credited.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The excess of open-cluster DYNAMICAL over PHOTOMETRIC mass is long known and is not discovered here.  It
  has been reported repeatedly from radial-velocity dispersions (a factor 2-10 depending on cluster) and is
  attributed in that literature to unresolved binaries, non-equilibrium expansion after gas expulsion, and
  tidal-tail contamination -- e.g. the Pleiades/Praesepe/Hyades dispersion studies and the Piskunov et al.
  (2007, 2008) tidal-versus-photometric mass comparisons.  What this script adds is (a) the proper-motion
  PERPENDICULAR estimator, (b) the mass-lever fit that separates a constant floor from a boost, and (c) the
  statement that the residual boost is B = 0.59 +- 0.14, which is BELOW Newton, not above it.

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT ON CANDIDATE 2 (the open-cluster rung)
======================================================================================================================
  MEASURED:  B = M_dyn/M_phot's mass-scaling coefficient = 0.589 +- 0.139 (bootstrap),
             with a fitted non-virial floor sigma_floor = 0.292 km/s, on 91 clusters older than 100 Myr and above 300 Msun.
             Raw median M_dyn/M_phot = 3.69 with 0.420 dex of scatter.

  PREDICTIONS:  Newton 1.000 | saturation law 1.269 (canonical) / 1.344 (alt) | isolated MOND 5.6

  [PASS] (1) a relation between MEASURED quantities
         yes -- proper-motion dispersion, half-number radius and summed member masses are all measured
  [PASS] (2) a_0 appears with a PREDICTED coefficient
         yes -- B(e) has nothing fitted once a_0 is fixed
  [FAIL] (3) holds across many systems to <= 0.1 dex
         NO -- the measured scatter is 0.42 dex, 4x the requirement, and the systematic floor (eta, half-mass vs half-number radius, IMF completeness) is 0.15-0.20 dex on its own
  [FAIL] (4) nobody has stated it
         the open-cluster dynamical/photometric mass excess is long published; only the estimator is new
  [PASS] (5) NOT a restatement of v^4 = G M_b a_0
         confirmed by execution in section 11

  >>> 3 of 5 criteria met.  THE OPEN-CLUSTER RUNG IS NOT A SECOND LAW.
  >>> WHAT IT DOES DECIDE, and this is a real result: ISOLATED MOND -- the boost that v^4 = G M_b a_0 alone
      implies, 5.6 for these clusters -- is excluded at 36 sigma.  Open clusters do NOT look
      dark-matter dominated, so the external-field effect must be switched on.  That confirms a standard,
      already-published ingredient of MOND; it is not new content.
  >>> WHAT IT CANNOT DECIDE: the saturation law's 0.10-0.13 dex boost against Newton's zero.  With the
      non-virial floor free the fit returns B = 0.59; with the floor forced to zero it returns 1.06.
      That 0.47 swing, from ONE modelling choice, is 4x the entire predicted signal.  The rung is blind.
  >>> AGAINST INTEREST, the central value sits BELOW Newton, not above it, and every systematic this script
      could identify (mass segregation, unseen low-mass stars, a King rather than Plummer profile) pushes it
      DOWN further, not up toward the law.

  UPSILON LEVER (measured by re-running at Upsilon x 1.5): d log B / d log Upsilon = -1.000.
  SHAPE STATEMENTS (flatness in mass, rise with R_gal) carry d/d log Upsilon = 0.000, but the R_gal lever on
  this sample is 3x too short to use.

